    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fatou Bensouda, the second Chief Prosecutor of the International Criminal Court, inherited a complicated legacy. The 1998 Rome Statute’s progressive approach to gender aroused hope that the ICC would conduct more sensitive and comprehensive prosecutions of sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV) than its ad hoc precursors. But, when Bensouda stepped into her role, her predecessor was leaving under a decade of criticism for his neglect, incompetence and indifference toward sexual and gender-based crimes. \r\n\r\nThe tide began to turn under Bensouda — the first African woman to be prosecutor of an international tribunal, and the first prosecutor to publish a comprehensive policy on sexual and gender-based crimes. What institutional and methodological changes did Bensouda make to the Office of the Prosecutor (OTP), and did they translate into material advancements at trial and beyond? Can progress on SGBV be attributed to particular policy changes, or were failures and successes more determined by external factors? Through this thesis, I have conducted a textual analysis of the cases, strategy documents and policy papers from both Bensouda’s and her predecessor Luis Moreno Ocampo’s terms, as well as through interviews with current and former staff members. My research suggests that although Bensouda implemented important reforms that facilitated substantial progress at trial, exogenous factors related to the ICC’s inherent institutional weaknesses both exacerbated the OTP’s failures and qualified its successes. Still, her term offers notable lessons for adapting to institutional challenges to best increase accountability for sexual and gender-based crimes under international criminal law.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
